      <p>
        Each region needs different evidence. Screening and build quality catch early failures. Life
        tests show wear-out. A model that assumes a constant failure rate, like the exponential, can miss
        both ends.
      </p>
      <p>
        Average lifetime alone can be misleading. Take a synthetic example: 70 units cycled to
        failure. Three models fit the data with nearly the same MTTF, about 22,000 cycles. Their
        predictions for when the first 10% fail differ by about four times. The fitted Weibull
        shape, β ≈ 2.2, says the failure rate rises with use. The exponential model assumes it
        stays flat, so it fits worst.
      </p>
